        heading: "What Doesn't Work (And What to Watch For)",
        content:
          "**Buying a more expensive 'VIP' or 'fast-track' ticket:** A pricier ticket gets you the exact same Door 1 entrance as a standard reserved ticket. Don't overpay for marketing language.\n\n**Arriving super early without a ticket:** If you turn up at 7 AM hoping to buy at the office when it opens, you'll wait in the ticket-office queue until 8:15 AM, then wait again in the entry queue. Reserving ahead is faster.\n\n**Hunting for a separate group or walk-up entrance:** There isn't one. Door 1 is the only way in, for individuals and organized groups alike.\n\n**Looking for Door 3 to collect a ticket:** Don't. Since CoopCulture took over ticketing in September 2025 there are no paper vouchers and nothing to pick up — your QR code goes straight to Door 1.\n\n**Visiting on the first Sunday of the month thinking it'll be easy because it's free:** The opposite. Free Sundays draw the largest crowds of the month, and there is no way to book the Uffizi's free ticket ahead — you have to queue at the ticket-office counter for it on the day. Queues can exceed 3-4 hours. If you want to save money, visit on a weekday and buy at the ticket office for €25 — you'll save time and sanity.\n\n**The bottom line:** There's no magic trick. The best strategy is a combination of reserving a time slot, smart timing, and seasonal awareness. Combine a reserved 8:15 AM slot with a Tuesday in October, and you'll walk into the Uffizi in under 5 minutes.",
